# The per-scope omission, unauthorized-attester, stale, conflicting-value and
# remaining counterfactual cases stay separate. They establish what must be
# shown before a record is kept, watched or automated over, and what is owed
# while it has not been; they authenticate no record and observe no execution.
# Full interface and evidence limits:
#   book-1/appendix/contracts/record-power-contract.md
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. The fixture is the subject, so it is loaded rather than
# inlined. Run:
#   ./verify.sh --only book-1/02-what-the-record-may-say.pins.nibli
#   ./verify.sh          # all substantive pins and formal contradiction checks
# Markdown consistency is reviewed separately; the pins do not certify prose.
#
# One entry per load-bearing sentence in the chapter. Private to the author; the
# reader never sees this file. If a verdict flips, the sentence it guards has
# started lying — fix the prose or the constitution, never the pin.

# WHAT FALSE MEANS HERE — three kinds, and only one is a verdict. A FALSE on a
# corpus name this KB never mentions is real but empty: nothing could have made
# it TRUE. A non-corpus name is not a FALSE at all — the run aborts, pins not
# trustworthy. And a well-formed query on an argument its relation never
# carries is a vacuous green that passes forever. Article 0a closed the
# ASSERTION side only — a widening fact now aborts the run instead of loading —
# but a QUERY on an unmentioned name still answers a clean FALSE, so kind one
# is untouched. Pin a FALSE only where the KB could have said TRUE, and say in
# the comment what would flip it.

:expect-pins 45

# 1.2 "Hano injured Ivo. A court judged Hano. Both of those are facts."
? injure(Hano, Ivo).
# => TRUE

? judge(Court, Hano).
# => TRUE

# 1.2 cont. "Hano is also a prisoner — but that is not a fact anyone recorded."
# TRUE, and reached only by derivation; the two refusals below prove the second half.
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

# 1.3 "You cannot simply declare someone guilty. There is no way to write it."
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
prisoner(Zed).

# 1.4 "Nor can you hand yourself the authority to judge."
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
authority(Pax).

# 1.3b "Nor can you write down that an offence was serious." — the third of the
# chapter's three cannot-write claims, and the only one that was unpinned.
:refuse reasoning /declared derived-only/
severe(Zed).

# 1.4 cont. "Either a seating body chose you, or you hold a public office.
#            Those are the only routes."
? public(Court).
# => TRUE

? authority(Court).
# => TRUE

# ...and the seating body need not be the electorate. Saba holds a seat the
# convocation gave — no office, no electorate entry — and answerability follows
# the same way. This is the route the sentence used to deny: it said "the
# electorate seated you" and "only two routes", stale since the second seating
# body was added. Saba isolates the third rule: nothing else confers it.
? choose(Convocation, Saba).
# => TRUE

? authority(Saba).
# => TRUE

# 1.5 "Koa documented something about Esa. ... Esa's credibility is untouched."
? capture(Koa, Esa).
# => TRUE

? false(Esa).
# => FALSE

# 1.5 cont. "Nor is Koa marked for having looked."
? false(Koa).
# => FALSE

# 1.6 "Three names sit on an amendment register. ... The list of what counts
#      as evidence is not on that register."
# This pin is DELIBERATELY the uncomfortable one. It is the chapter's closing
# admission. A TRUE would change the label verdict and require a rewrite, but
# would still not prove that an `admits` edit was semantically entrenched.
? permanent(Art_Floor).
# => TRUE

? permanent(Art_Evidence).
# => FALSE

# 1.6b "they cannot. Not *may not*. Cannot. There is nowhere to put it."
# [v0.9] THIS SENTENCE WAS FALSE UNTIL ARTICLE 0a. nibli's lexicon is
# fail-closed, so an invented word never loaded — but `rich` is a real corpus
# name and `rich(Adam).` answered TRUE. The closure was at nibli's vocabulary,
# not at this book's. `admits` moved it here, and these two pins are the whole
# of the chapter's opening claim: a name off the list is refused, and adding one
# has to be a visible edit above the first use.
:refuse reasoning /not admitted vocabulary/
rich(Adam).

:refuse reasoning /not admitted vocabulary/
dangerous(Adam).

# and the closure is EXTENSIONAL — conclusions still derive
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

# An accepted raw accusation is not an adjudicated finding about the exposure.
# Keep the assertion and both verdicts: the protection must survive this write.
? prisoner(Iris).
# => FALSE

:accept
deceive(Iris, Tove).

? prisoner(Iris).
# => FALSE

# "A person has a family" — one word, buried in a list of attributes. For
# Hano's family label is recorded only to prove it changes no placement
# result; severity and the separate placement-home record decide the route.
? fit(Hano, Homestay).
# => TRUE

? building(Homestay, Hano).
# => TRUE

:accept
family(Hano).

? fit(Hano, Homestay).
# => TRUE

? building(Homestay, Hano).
# => TRUE

? building(LowSec, Hano).
# => FALSE

# 1.3c THE PAIRING IS THE RULE. "An offence is severe when two of those stand
# together in the record: an attack and a cruelty landing on the same person,
# or either of them landing on one person while an injury lands on another."
# The gloss this replaced — "any two of the three are present" — was refuted by
# exactly these verdicts: attack and cruelty toward DIFFERENT people derive
# nothing, and the same pair on one person derives severity. Fresh names;
# effects disjoint from everything below — severe feeds placement only through
# prisoner, and Probe is nobody's prisoner. Unscoped for the file's usual
# reason: each accept is the premise of the query under it.
#
# The court facts below were added on 2026-09-16, when severity became
# court-bound: it reads the Court's judgment and its cited case besides the
# pairing. They are here so this exhibit keeps demonstrating what it is about —
# the PAIRING — rather than silently becoming a demonstration of the new
# conjuncts. That severity is now court-bound has its own case, in
# tests/pins/red-team/an-accusation-nobody-signed.
:accept
judge(Court, Probe).

:accept
cite(Court, ProbeCase, Probe).

:accept
attack(Probe, Vic1).

:accept
cruel(Probe, Vic2).

? severe(Probe).
# => FALSE

:accept
cruel(Probe, Vic1).

? severe(Probe).
# => FALSE

# A raw Court-failure report is not a qualified case-specific disposition.
# Both original cases and both before/after questions remain.
? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Lalo).
# => TRUE

broken(Court).

? prisoner(Hano).
# => TRUE

? prisoner(Lalo).
# => TRUE

# 1.7 "Someone is a person." — the entry, and the two ways it gets written.
#
# THESE MUST BE LAST IN THE FILE AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC. An accepted assertion
# loads into the KB and stays there. `person(Ghost).` puts a name on the roster
# with every floor right, `travel`, and a public body owing it things; the
# `free` route does the same by a longer path. Any pin querying the roster after
# this point measures a polluted KB and passes for the wrong reason —
# `rights-floor.pins.nibli:285` records a real vacuous green from exactly this.
# Put new queries ABOVE this block.
#
# DO NOT CONVERT THESE TO `:accept-scoped`. Each accepted statement here is the
# premise of the query under it, not a control — scoped, it is retracted before
# that query runs and the verdict flips from TRUE to FALSE (measured 2026-08-01
# against nibli 425567b; the accept itself still passes, so the loss surfaces
# only below it). The conversion does not fail quietly — it fails as a finding,
# which reads like the design regressed, and this file's rule is never to fix
# the pin.
#
# It does not reach the other thirteen files: each gets a fresh KB. Proof —
# `rights-floor.pins.nibli` runs first and its controls would make
# `travel(Bela)` FALSE, yet `24-findings-about-people.pins.nibli` pins it TRUE in the same
# green run.

# "Someone's sentence is finished" is ON the list, and it confers personhood.
# This is the pin the chapter's decision turned on: the list already reached the
# roster before anybody decided whether `person` belonged on it.
:accept
free(Wraith).

? person(Wraith).
# => TRUE

# And the entry itself is writable, with nothing asked and nobody consulted.
# "a name belonging to nobody acquires every right on the floor ... the freedom
#  to move ... Nothing anywhere asks who this is."
:accept
person(Ghost).

? travel(Ghost).
# => TRUE

# ── The child with nobody ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "It could be written, because a birth is a fact somebody reports and the list
# admits it" — the exposure this chapter concedes, asked of the thinnest record.
? person(Nell).
# => TRUE

# "the record admits no word for what the child is" — the refusal happens at
# the closed vocabulary, before the rule loads, not after somebody notices.
:refuse syntax /unknown predicate "vulnerable"/
vulnerable(Nell).

# And the conclusions this chapter keeps apart stay apart for the child too:
# nothing about one birth entry concludes anything about them.
? false(Nell).
# => FALSE
